  "worked_solution": "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{3}).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}=\\frac{3}{x}\\,\u27f9\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $e$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{3})}{5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3}u\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}{\\int }_{0}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
